[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Well, hello there. I\'m in VR again, but more&nbsp; specifically, I\'m in a Valve Steam Frame. The&nbsp;&nbsp; Valve Steam Frame is real and it is finally coming&nbsp; out. This VR standalone headset/game console is&nbsp;&nbsp; $1,059 to start unless you choose to get a higher&nbsp; config model and I want to tell you all about my&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'experience wearing it. Um, because I\'ve done a lot&nbsp; with it. But this little thing is actually kind&nbsp;&nbsp; of a doorway to a lot of weird directions that&nbsp; Valve and even the gaming industry are heading in&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because it\'s not just a VR headset. It\'s kind of&nbsp; a portable Steam Deck for your face. I said that&nbsp;&nbsp; when I visited Valve last November, but it\'s got&nbsp; the ability to play 2D Steam games [music] as well&nbsp;&nbsp; as VR games. And that\'s because not only are the&nbsp; game controllers on here mapped [music] to match a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Steam controller\'s buttons, so you can play that,&nbsp; but you can just download and play stuff from your&nbsp;&nbsp; Steam library right onto this headset. But your&nbsp; mileage is going to vary. Now, at first glance,&nbsp;&nbsp; Steam Frame looks like a MetaQuest or lots of&nbsp; other VR headsets from the past. It is small.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s smaller feeling and lighter feeling than the&nbsp; Metaquest 3. Um, and it has a slightly different&nbsp;&nbsp; design. The battery pack is on the back and on&nbsp; the front is the visor which distributes weight&nbsp;&nbsp; better. But there\'s also a separately sold for&nbsp; $59 ergonomic pack which has a head strap which&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'goes over the top and it also has these grippy&nbsp; uh things for the controllers so you don\'t uh&nbsp;&nbsp; maybe drop them as easily. It does come with&nbsp; these little hand loop straps right out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'box. I think this offers better looser control&nbsp; without the grippy things. The grippy things&nbsp;&nbsp; are better for things if you\'re playing fitness&nbsp; games and stuff like that. But I actually found&nbsp;&nbsp; it hard to move my hands in. Um, the one thing&nbsp; about this headset that I immediately thought&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was a challenge was glasses fit. Now, you could&nbsp; put your glasses into a steam frame. I thought&nbsp;&nbsp; it was incredibly tight and uncomfortable and&nbsp; um, I don\'t like that at all. You can also get&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prescription inserts, which I did get to test out&nbsp; made by Zeni. They\'re about $68. They are great,&nbsp;&nbsp; but they come in a limited set of prescriptions&nbsp; and they don\'t technically support my range,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'although they did make a custom pair that do. I\'m&nbsp; concerned about the fact that this may not fit&nbsp;&nbsp; everyone\'s eyes, and that\'s something that is true&nbsp; in a lot of VR and AR stuff across the industry.&nbsp;&nbsp; Once you get past that, the viewing experience on&nbsp; the Steam Frame is very nice. It\'s not reinventing&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anything really, and there\'s a lot of familiar VR&nbsp; feelings. It\'s 2160x 2160 peri LCD display with a&nbsp;&nbsp; refresh rate that goes up to 144 hertz which is&nbsp; really nice but that refresh rate varies right&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'now if you know about VR gaming and gaming in&nbsp; general when you actually play the games on this&nbsp;&nbsp; and I\'ll get into that in a second. It also has an&nbsp; ability to uh widen or reduce the distance between&nbsp;&nbsp; the lenses for IPD. And it has eyetracking,&nbsp; which is very unusual for a headset this size,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which Valve only uses for foviated rendering,&nbsp; which is where it only shows the stuff that&nbsp;&nbsp; you\'re looking at in great detail, while the rest&nbsp; out of your direct view is a little fuzzy, but you&nbsp;&nbsp; don\'t notice it. Now, it\'s used for both fiated&nbsp; rendering on the headset and something Valve calls&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fiated streaming, which is when you connect to&nbsp; your PC using this little dongle. You can stream&nbsp;&nbsp; from the Steam Frame. You actually don\'t need&nbsp; this to stream. You could use Wi-Fi as well, but&nbsp;&nbsp; this is meant to have a direct um six gigahertz&nbsp; connection. It\'s a more reliable connection and&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they also use a technology called fiated streaming&nbsp; which again uses eyetracking to just focus on the&nbsp;&nbsp; stuff you\'re doing for the higher resolution&nbsp; saving that stream\'s bandwidth a little bit.'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now I will admit I am not a PC gamer and&nbsp; actually I\'ve not even really been using this for&nbsp;&nbsp; PC streaming yet. What I\'ve been looking at is all&nbsp; of its standalone features. Because let\'s face it,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for over $1,000, there are a lot of different&nbsp; VR headsets you can plug into your PC to use&nbsp;&nbsp; for Steam gaming, but there aren\'t that many&nbsp; standalones. And I thought testing this as a&nbsp;&nbsp; standalone is the really unique factor. Now,&nbsp; getting back into the VR part of things. This&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is running a Snapdragon Gen 8 series 3 processor.&nbsp; It\'s not using a Qualcomm XR chip like a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp; the landscape is using. I don\'t really know&nbsp; how that stands in terms of performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but it feels a lot like the MetaQuest in terms&nbsp; of performance. In other words, it\'s not really&nbsp;&nbsp; pushing a lot of brand new territory. And a lot of&nbsp; the games that run on this at the moment that are&nbsp;&nbsp; Valve verified for Steam Frame, which is there&nbsp; about a hundred or so so far, those VR games&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are mostly ports of stuff you can already play on&nbsp; Quest that I have played before. 11 Table Tennis,&nbsp;&nbsp; um, Walkabout Miniolf, and they\'re fine. And in&nbsp; fact, a lot of the VR games at the moment default&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to 72 hertz, which is not that high a refresh&nbsp; rate in VR. It could be that those games need&nbsp;&nbsp; to further optimize. And the headset has a lot&nbsp; of deep settings, which I almost got lost in,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but you could potentially tweak and make&nbsp; different performance things. Your mileage&nbsp;&nbsp; may vary. The default is to go to 72 hertz,&nbsp; and I\'m sure that may change, and some may&nbsp;&nbsp; perform better. The biggest showcase that Valve&nbsp; is showing on this, let\'s just get right to the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'point. I played Half-Life: Alyx on this. Yes, that&nbsp; is right. Half-Life: Alyx is on Steam Frame in a&nbsp;&nbsp; beta build that I got to try that\'s not in mass&nbsp; release yet. That is clearly Valve\'s bleeding&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'edge demonstration of what this could do. Now,&nbsp; Half-Life: Alyx on this is definitely a step down&nbsp;&nbsp; in graphics. The the frame rate for animations is&nbsp; lower. Even though when you look around the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything feels very snappy and fast moving. Uh,&nbsp; a lot of the hand movements feel a little wonkier.&nbsp;&nbsp; Um, some of the animations are zipped up, but I&nbsp; really felt like I was being absorbed in this game&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'again. And heck, it\'s playing on my face. That\'s&nbsp; pretty incredible. When I played Half-Life: Alyx&nbsp; with these controllers, I was really impressed&nbsp; because not only did the controller mappings work,&nbsp;&nbsp; but it it noticed my finger motions over here.&nbsp; And that\'s because this controller is covered&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in capacitive touch sensors, not only all&nbsp; across the face, but also on the grip. So,&nbsp;&nbsp; I was really surprised that if I moved my pinky&nbsp; finger or my ring finger, the game knew. And there&nbsp;&nbsp; are a bunch of other familiar buttons on here.&nbsp; There are three different triggers on the back,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'two at the top, one on the side for grip. There&nbsp; are dual analog sticks, but there\'s also a D-pad,&nbsp;&nbsp; and there are four buttons on the other side.&nbsp; Basically, all that mapping into what the Steam&nbsp;&nbsp; controller is like, or the Steam Deck, minus the&nbsp; two touch pads below. But you could basically play&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'almost all Steam games with that button mapping&nbsp; provided the game performed well enough on this.&nbsp;&nbsp; So as a VR headset, again, it\'s got eyetracking,&nbsp; but the pass through cameras are black and white.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That\'s kind of a bummer because the MetaQuest&nbsp; has color stuff and Apple Vision Pro has amazing&nbsp;&nbsp; stuff, but that\'s $3,600, $3,500. But this does&nbsp; have a plug-in module. This is a modular headset.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There is a camera module, Arcturus Vision,&nbsp; that\'s $150 and plugs right in here and gives&nbsp;&nbsp; you high resolution color that is a big step up.&nbsp; It\'s not quite vision pro style, but I was able&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to actually read things on my phone and see&nbsp; things in the room much better. And you could&nbsp;&nbsp; even record spatial video or use it for things&nbsp; like 3D scanning called Gaussian splats. Um,&nbsp;&nbsp; there\'s a lot of possibilities there and it points&nbsp; to how modular the Steam Frame could be. What&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'else do you want to put into that port? Uh, also&nbsp; Valve allows all of these things to be detachable.&nbsp;&nbsp; Magnetic face plates. These straps come off.&nbsp; The battery here could be detached. This whole&nbsp;&nbsp; piece could be taken off and you could put on a&nbsp; different front fit. There\'s apparently another&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Pro headset strap coming with a hot swappable&nbsp; battery. So, I think there could be all sorts&nbsp;&nbsp; of tinkering that could happen, but that assumes&nbsp; that you might know how to do the tinkering or&nbsp;&nbsp; that the tinkering stuff actually arrives. In that&nbsp; tinkering spirit, this actually runs Linux and you&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can exit into a desktop mode. So, you could run&nbsp; something like Firefox, you could try Web XR,&nbsp;&nbsp; you could download all sorts of other apps&nbsp; or sideloadad apps or or who knows what.&nbsp;&nbsp; There\'s a big open road that I\'ve only dipped&nbsp; my toes into. And you can run any game on this,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but it\'ll let you fail out, too. So, it\'s not like&nbsp; you just have to run the ones that are actually&nbsp;&nbsp; optimized. I tried a lot of different things,&nbsp; including original VR games that I played in&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'HTC Vive a decade ago. Uh, Magic Man\'s Head games,&nbsp; um, Adventure Time, which you can\'t even get now,&nbsp;&nbsp; and it doesn\'t run. I tried playing the, uh, Rick&nbsp; and Morty game that had come out a whole bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp; years ago, that ran at such a slow frame rate&nbsp; that I got nauseous and I just took it off. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you want to stick to the ones that are&nbsp; properly optimized, but you could tinker. Now, how is it as a steam deck? That is&nbsp; interesting because when you put this on,&nbsp;&nbsp; if you get comfortable with that&nbsp; nice wide 110 degree field of view,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it does feel like a little viewing experience&nbsp; on the go. Kind of like the Apple Vision Pro,&nbsp;&nbsp; but at a lower price where you could play your&nbsp; games on a plane or somewhere else. I downloaded&nbsp;&nbsp; a whole bunch of games to try onto this, as many&nbsp; as I could find. Many of them do not run because&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s running at a lower performance than the Steam&nbsp; Deck, which is already several years old. This&nbsp;&nbsp; ARM chip is a stepping stone. Seeing how&nbsp; Steam games might run on a lower power processor,&nbsp;&nbsp; which could lead to possibly having Steam devices&nbsp; that run on some tablety type thing or a smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'handheld that may indeed be part of Valve\'s&nbsp; mission here where this is kind of a bridge&nbsp;&nbsp; device. But at the moment, expect games that are&nbsp; more like uh Cuphead, Balatro, Hollow&nbsp;&nbsp;Knight. Uh those all played fine. I tried games&nbsp; that weren\'t officially approved. Um that also ran&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'well. Sektori, which is a very fast uh trigger,&nbsp; you know, shooter game, uh worked marvelously.&nbsp;&nbsp; Baby Steps, which is a more graphically intense&nbsp; game, I could play it on here. That was really&nbsp;&nbsp; cool. Um there are other games like Elden Ring&nbsp; that were totally unplayable. Ratchet and Clank,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'forget it. So again, you\'re going to have to hunt&nbsp; around or wait for more games to get optimized. Now, this gets to the point of early products&nbsp; and Valve. Valve is a company that&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'likes to launch things experimentally and then&nbsp; fix them over time and actually has a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp; good track record of that. I mean, the Steam&nbsp; Deck when it launched was very buggy and then&nbsp;&nbsp; it became fantastic over time. Steam Machine so&nbsp; far over the last few months has gotten better,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'although there\'s still a lot of stuff I\'d like&nbsp; to see it get. This feels like it needs a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp; updates, but I wonder about when they\'ll be added&nbsp; and how. For instance, you can\'t stream games from&nbsp;&nbsp; the Steam machine yet, although I\'d love to, but&nbsp; that apparently is coming soon. This doesn\'t do&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'handtracking, although Valve says that\'s also&nbsp; probably going to be worked on down the road.&nbsp;&nbsp; And there are a lot of games that seem to kind&nbsp; of uh crap out and get weird and and have a few&nbsp;&nbsp; different quirks. Plus, expect more games to be&nbsp; added to the library or to develop compatibility&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'um improvements because Valve does a lot of&nbsp; tinkering with some of the tools to convert.&nbsp;&nbsp; They\'re optimistic that even classic games&nbsp; that have not been updated should eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'be able to run on this thing. I love the idea of&nbsp; this. At this moment, for $1,000, it is really&nbsp;&nbsp; experimental and the landscape is changing so&nbsp; fast. I mean, there are a lot of devices that&nbsp;&nbsp; are even more glasses like that are going to be&nbsp; like VR headsets in the next few years. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are some glasses that are going to have displays&nbsp; that are going to feel like VR headsets, like the&nbsp;&nbsp; XReal Project Aura that I saw that\'s running&nbsp; Android XR. Where does this more traditional&nbsp;&nbsp; VR headset fit in with a battery, by the way,&nbsp; battery life that is not good. I got two hours&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at best out of this and felt I always needed to&nbsp; recharge, which makes it very unlike a Steam Deck.&nbsp;&nbsp; definitely a your mileage may vary device and keep&nbsp; that in mind. But I hope this improves and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it will and I\'m going to keep checking it out&nbsp; over time and give updates because this is part&nbsp;&nbsp; of a transformation that Valve is currently doing.&nbsp; I just wish there was a little more finesse. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'by the way, I wish there was a case because&nbsp; there isn\'t and I had to get my own to get&nbsp;&nbsp; it to fit in because delicate. I want to&nbsp; coddle it. Anyway, those are my thoughts on&nbsp;&nbsp; Steam Frame right now. Thanks for watching&nbsp; and let me know your thoughts down below.'
